<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Yan (An-Shi rebellion)</h1><p class="st-meta">756 CE – 763 CE · capital Luoyang</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The rebel empire of An Lushan and Shi Siming, whose eight-year war nearly destroyed the Tang and left China's north-east in the hands of warlords.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-lushan">An Lushan</a>, a general of Sogdian and Turkic descent and favourite of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-xuanzong-of-tang">Emperor Xuanzong</a>, commanded three frontier armies in the north-east. Fearing the chief minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yang-guozhong">Yang Guozhong</a>, he rebelled at the end of 755, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> and early in 756 proclaimed himself emperor of Great Yan, capturing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> that summer.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The state was a military regime of frontier soldiers, including many Sogdian, Turkic and Khitan troops. It never governed securely: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-lushan">An Lushan</a> was murdered by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-qingxu">An Qingxu</a> in 757, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> forces, aided by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> cavalry, retook both capitals that year. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shi-siming">Shi Siming</a> killed An Qingxu in 759, retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> and was in turn murdered by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shi-chaoyi">Shi Chaoyi</a> in 761.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shi-chaoyi">Shi Chaoyi</a> was defeated and killed himself in 763 as his generals surrendered. Millions had died or been displaced, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a> survived only by leaving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a> provinces to hereditary military governors, while the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/tibetan-empire">Tibetan Empire</a> seized the north-west.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/yan-an-shi-rebellion.jpg" alt="Yan (An-Shi rebellion)" width="320" height="226"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:An_Lushan_Rebellion.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>756 CE – 763 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Luoyang</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>An Lushan, Shi Siming, Shi Chaoyi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Zoroastrianism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese, Sogdian, Turkic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Great Yan, Yan (756-763)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=760&amp;focus=yan-an-shi-rebellion" aria-label="Yan (An-Shi rebellion) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7535 923 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Yan (An-Shi rebellion) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7855 1179L7771 1192"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7771 1192h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7855 1179h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7855 1179h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Luoyang. <a href="/atlas?year=760&amp;focus=yan-an-shi-rebellion">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
